<!--
Placeholders:
  {{PACKAGE_VERSIONS}}  — one <PackageVersion> per distinct package in the solution

Repo root. Central package management: one place declares every version, the projects declare
only what they use.

THIS IS A MIGRATION, NOT A FILE DROP. Writing this file without editing the projects breaks the
build. The full sequence:

  1. Collect every <PackageReference Include="X" Version="Y" /> across the solution.
  2. Where the same package appears at more than one version, ASK which wins. Report the drift.
  3. Write one <PackageVersion> per package here.
  4. Edit every .csproj: remove the Version attribute, keep everything else
     (PrivateAssets, IncludeAssets, ExcludeAssets, the child elements).
  5. Delete every obj/ directory, then run `dotnet restore` and confirm it succeeds. The
     project.assets.json left over from the pre-migration state makes restore fail with errors
     that point at packages rather than at the migration, which is the wrong trail entirely.

NEVER PUT TWO CONSECUTIVE HYPHENS IN A COMMENT.
XML forbids the sequence inside a comment, so MSBuild cannot load the file. It is easy to hit here,
because the skill asks you to justify every exception and command-line flags are the natural way to
name one. Write the flag as prose instead: "locked mode", not the flag spelling.

The failure gives you nothing to work with. `dotnet restore` reports

  MSB4181: The "RestoreTask" task returned false but did not log an error

with no file and no line. If a build starts failing right after this file is edited, validate it
before investigating anything else:

  dotnet msbuild <any-project> -getProperty:TargetFramework

That surfaces the real XML error, naming the file and the line.

After the migration a project reference looks like:

  <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />

CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled pins transitive dependencies too, which is what closes the
"it resolved differently today" gap. Turn it off only if it breaks a restore you cannot otherwise
fix, and say why.

CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled is set to false because NuGet defaults it to true, and the
default leaves the escape hatch open: any project can add VersionOverride to a PackageReference
and go back to its own version, silently, in the one file nobody rereads. That is the exact drift
this migration was run to remove, so leaving it available makes the file a convention rather than
a control. With it false, restore fails with NU1013 and names the project — a build error, which
is a conversation, instead of a divergence nobody sees for six months.

This is a real constraint, not a formality, so SAY IT IN THE PHASE 6 REPORT alongside the file:
after this, a project that genuinely needs a different version raises it here or states its case,
and the answer is usually a second PackageVersion under a condition rather than an override. If a
repo turns out to have a legitimate exception the team will not give up, turn it back on and
record which project needs it and why — an override nobody can explain is the failure mode; an
override with a reason next to it is not.

Delete this header before writing the file.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
    <CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled>false</CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
{{PACKAGE_VERSIONS}}
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
